into the Byzantine Empire."},{"sourceSlug":"Capua-Diocese","sourceName":"Capua Diocese","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"Monasticism","targetName":"Monasticism","context":"The development of monasticism in the region was influenced by Victor of Capua's writings and practices."},{"sourceSlug":"Southern-Italy","sourceName":"Southern Italy","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Christianity","targetName":"Christianity","context":"The Christianization of southern Italy paved the way for its eventual dominance over the pagan cultures of the region."}],"places":[{"name":"Capua","role":"Diocese"},{"name":"Southern Italy","role":"Region"},{"name":"Byzantine Empire","role":"Empire"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Victor of Capua","type":"Reference article","slug":"victor-of-capua"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q7926487","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Victor_of_Capua"],"tags":["Religious Figures","comparative-religion","doctrine-development","cultural-diffusion"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"","summary":"Victor of Capua (died 554 CE) was a 6th-century Italian bishop and biblical scholar — Bishop of Capua in Campania — who is primarily known for a significant contribution to biblical textual history: he is associated with the 'Fuldensis Gospel Harmony', a Latin revision of Tatian's Diatessaron (the famous 2nd-century Gospel harmony compiled by the Syriac Christian scholar Tatian) that Victor commissioned or composed. The Codex Fuldensis — a 6th-century Latin New Testament manuscript housed at the Abbey of Fulda (from which it takes its name) — includes a Latin version of a Gospel harmony that Victor prepared in 545 CE, with a preface explaining his editorial work: he noted that he had discovered a Latin Diatessaron that had been poorly rendered and had revised and reorganised it to align with the canonical four-gospel structure.

Victor's Gospel harmony is significant for several reasons in biblical scholarship: it preserves elements of an ancient Gospel harmony tradition (Tatian's Diatessaron was one of the most widely used Christian texts before the four-gospel canon was fully established, especially in Syriac and Eastern Christianity), it demonstrates the continued interest in Gospel harmonisation in Western Christianity three centuries after Tatian, and it is evidence for the circulation of Diatessaron-related materials in Latin Christianity that had largely moved away from harmonisation toward the canonical four-gospel text.

Victor's scholarly activity places him in the tradition of 6th-century Italian ecclesiastical scholars — the generation that included Cassiodorus, Boethius, and Pope Gregory the Great — who worked to preserve, revise, and transmit the texts of the Christian tradition during the turbulent period of Ostrogothic rule and the Byzantine reconquest. His work on the Gospel text shows a bishop engaged in active literary and textual scholarship at a time when the institutional structures for such work were under stress from military and political disruption.","subjects":["Biblical Scholarship","Early Christianity","Church History","Classical Era","Italy","Biblical Text","Medieval Era","6th Century"],"frameworks":["RELIGIOUS_INTERPRETATION","STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"Victor of Capua's efforts to spread Christianity throughout southern Italy had a lasting impact on the region's spiritual landscape.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
